    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n23799" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δέκᾰ</span></b> , οἱ, αἱ, τά,
		indecl., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>ten,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Il.
				2.372
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Od.
				9.160
			</span></i>, etc.; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οἱ δ.</span>”
				<i>the Ten,</i>
				 <i><span class="en">
					Isoc.
					18.6
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἡ τῶν δ. τυραννίς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Arist.
					<u>Ath.</u>
					41.2
				</span></i>
			; also <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οἱ δ.</span> the <i>Attic
				Orators,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Philostr.
				<u>VS</u>
				2.1.14
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὰ δέκα [ἔτη] ἀφ᾽
			ἥβης</span> those who are <i>ten</i> years past <i><span class="en">
				20
			</span></i> (the age of military service), <i><span class="en">
				X.
				<u>HG</u>
				3.4.23
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δ. ἄνδρες,</span>
			= Lat. <i>decemviri,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				App.
				<u>Hann.</u>
				56
			</span></i>: compds. (not in early writers, but usu. in Hellenistic Gr.)
				<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δεκᾰ-είς</span></b> , <i><span class="en">
				<u>Tab.Heracl.</u>
				2.34
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Plu.
				<u>Num.</u>
				3
			</span></i>:</div>
